/*
 * kotd-data.js — hand-authored kanji dataset for "Kanji of the Day"
 * (kotd.html / kotd.js). Distinct from data.js, which is word-level.
 *
 * Two blocks:
 *   ids 1-227   — N4/N5 textbook kanji (the original set).
 *   ids 228-504 — everyday kanji seen in daily life (signage, transit, shops,
 *                 food, weather, health, housing, forms, calendar terms,
 *                 devices), not scoped to a single JLPT level. Tagged with a
 *                 `category` (e.g. "日常・案内") on top of `level`.
 * The "singles 1-197 / compounds 198-227" grouping described in earlier
 * revisions of this file only holds for the first block — the second block
 * interleaves singles and compounds by category instead.
 *
 * Level tags follow the standard old-JLPT Level 4/3 -> N5/N4 kanji split
 * used across most study references for the first block. A handful of very
 * common everyday kanji (colors, vehicles, some family/place words) sit
 * right on that boundary and different publishers place them a level apart —
 * treat the N5/N4 split as a reasonable, commonly-used convention rather
 * than a single official source of truth. The everyday block uses N3 (or
 * occasionally N4) loosely, as a rough difficulty hint rather than a claim
 * about the official JLPT kanji list for that level.
 *
 * RAW entry shapes:
 *   type: "single"   -> { id, type, kanji, onyomi, kunyomi, meaning, meaningEn, level, category?, example }
 *   type: "compound" -> { id, type, word, reading, meaning, meaningEn, level, category?, parts: [id, id, ...], example }
 *     - onyomi/kunyomi: kana strings ("" if none for that reading type)
 *     - meaning: Indonesian gloss; meaningEn: English gloss
 *     - level: "N5" | "N4" | "N3" | "N2" | "N1"
 *     - category: optional 日常 theme label (everyday block only), rendered
 *       as a second badge by kotd.js
 *     - example: { jp, reading, meaning } — meaning is "Indonesian. (English.)"
 *     - parts (compound only): ids of the "single" entries the word is built from,
 *       used by kotd.js to render a "built from X + Y" breakdown
 *
 * `id` is a stable key independent of array position — used by compound
 * entries' `parts` references and by the "sudah paham" (known-card) set in
 * localStorage (see kotd.js).
 */
